        <h1 class="text-3xl font-serif font-bold text-charcoal-900 mb-3">Microfiber vs. Genuine Leather: A Shop Owner's Guide</h1>
        <p class="text-charcoal-400 text-sm mb-8">2026-10-04 · Jeff · 4 min read</p>
        <div class="text-charcoal-700 leading-relaxed space-y-5">
          <p>When a customer asks for "leather seats," they usually don't know which leather they mean. Neither does half the industry quoting them. Here's the practical breakdown we give our shop partners.</p>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-serif font-bold text-charcoal-900 pt-2">What microfiber actually is</h2>
          <p>Microfiber leather is a 3D non-woven structure of micro-fibers bonded with polyurethane. It imitates the fiber structure of real hide — which is why it feels closer to genuine leather than classic PU, and why it outlasts standard PVC in abrasion tests. Our microfiber grades run 0.8–1.2mm at 140–155cm width.</p>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-serif font-bold text-charcoal-900 pt-2">Where each one wins</h2>
          <p><b>Microfiber wins</b> on high-wear surfaces: seat bolsters, door panel inserts, steering wheels. It handles sweat, UV and repeated cleaning better, and at 0.8mm (our MC Mousse grade) it stitches beautifully on tight curves.</p>
          <p><b>PVC wins</b> on price-sensitive fleet work. At 1.2mm with 30+ OEM-matched colors, it cleans with a wet rag and takes abuse — the reason taxi and fleet programs spec it.</p>
          <p><b>Genuine leather wins</b> when the customer is paying for the story. Top-grain Nappa at 1.1–1.35mm breathes, ages, and smells like nothing synthetic can. For luxury restoration jobs, it's the only right answer.</p>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-serif font-bold text-charcoal-900 pt-2">The quoting mistake to avoid</h2>
          <p>Don't quote "leather" as one line item. Quote the material by application: microfiber on wear surfaces, PVC on budget builds, genuine on premium packages. Your customer sees three clear options — and you protect your margin on the middle one.</p>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-serif font-bold text-charcoal-900 pt-2">One compliance note</h2>
          <p>Whatever you quote, make sure it passes REACH and formaldehyde testing. Our series are SGS-tested — reports are downloadable on our Marketplace page. If your supplier can't produce those, that's your answer.</p>
        </div>
